WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.497
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:03.497 --> 00:00:04.330
LIAM MAXWELL: Great.

00:00:04.330 --> 00:00:05.560
Thanks very much.

00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:07.010
Thank you very much for coming.

00:00:07.010 --> 00:00:07.840
I'm Liam Maxwell.

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:09.460
I'm the national
technology adviser

00:00:09.460 --> 00:00:12.890
for the British government.

00:00:12.890 --> 00:00:15.760
One of the things we
have talked about a lot,

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:20.150
and this morning was
really strong about culture

00:00:20.150 --> 00:00:22.832
and the use of Open as culture
and how you deliver change.

00:00:22.832 --> 00:00:24.290
And so what I'm
going to talk about

00:00:24.290 --> 00:00:27.770
is how we delivered change
within a large organization

00:00:27.770 --> 00:00:29.960
using Open as the dynamic.

00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:32.600
Now, it is one of
those things that you

00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:34.980
get a load of neologisms
in government,

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:37.670
so people suddenly start
thinking up new words which

00:00:37.670 --> 00:00:40.790
become the word of the year.

00:00:40.790 --> 00:00:43.430
I sensed on Wednesday,
really, people

00:00:43.430 --> 00:00:46.250
were talking a lot
about lift and shift.

00:00:46.250 --> 00:00:48.740
And I think one of the things
that we found when we started

00:00:48.740 --> 00:00:52.550
in government was people started
talking about Agile as a thing.

00:00:52.550 --> 00:00:56.510
And I'm here to say, be
careful about those neologisms

00:00:56.510 --> 00:00:59.960
and how they're used, because
if you introduce a concept

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:02.810
like "Agile is a good
thing" into government,

00:01:02.810 --> 00:01:06.740
people start building huge
things out of agileness,

00:01:06.740 --> 00:01:10.820
and people start building
infrastructure in an agile way.

00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:12.770
And I remember
once finding myself

00:01:12.770 --> 00:01:15.830
in a presentation where somebody
described their approach to it

00:01:15.830 --> 00:01:20.270
and their approach to change
is agilution, which is Agile

00:01:20.270 --> 00:01:23.330
but done at their own pace.

00:01:23.330 --> 00:01:26.540
It was strange, because it
didn't actually understand

00:01:26.540 --> 00:01:29.220
what Agile, TM, really means.

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:33.380
So part of this is about talking
about the culture of what

00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:34.190
we do.

00:01:34.190 --> 00:01:36.650
And really at the
bottom is what you

00:01:36.650 --> 00:01:40.520
find when you start moving
and changing an organization,

00:01:40.520 --> 00:01:44.150
and how we used Open
as a dynamic for that.

00:01:44.150 --> 00:01:46.640
So using Open to [INAUDIBLE].

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:48.410
So imagine, if you
will, that you've

00:01:48.410 --> 00:01:51.500
discovered yourself
unexpectedly winning an election

00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:53.510
and starting to
run a government.

00:01:53.510 --> 00:01:54.980
What do you do at
that point, when

00:01:54.980 --> 00:01:56.750
you start to look
around you and how you

00:01:56.750 --> 00:01:58.577
can change what you're doing?

00:01:58.577 --> 00:01:59.660
So our challenge was this.

00:01:59.660 --> 00:02:04.790
In 2009, government IT cost
16 billion pounds in the UK.

00:02:04.790 --> 00:02:08.210
Often I've found people say it's
quite worrying when you go over

00:02:08.210 --> 00:02:09.250
about a million pounds.

00:02:09.250 --> 00:02:11.630
It's quite difficult to
imagine what that money is.

00:02:11.630 --> 00:02:15.660
16 billion is quite
a lot of money.

00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:17.930
So there's 1% of our
economy was spent

00:02:17.930 --> 00:02:20.480
on the administrative
functioning of technology

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:21.620
in government.

00:02:21.620 --> 00:02:23.090
A lot of money.

00:02:23.090 --> 00:02:27.080
And my minister had a
particularly interesting way

00:02:27.080 --> 00:02:28.700
of describing that.

00:02:28.700 --> 00:02:31.070
It wasn't good.

00:02:31.070 --> 00:02:33.690
It didn't work for people.

00:02:33.690 --> 00:02:35.750
And we didn't do the
most important things.

00:02:35.750 --> 00:02:37.670
When you find yourself
in a new place,

00:02:37.670 --> 00:02:40.100
you have to map where
you are, and you

00:02:40.100 --> 00:02:42.240
have to measure where you are.

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:45.570
Unless you can work out
where you are qualitatively,

00:02:45.570 --> 00:02:48.260
where in the cycle you
are, what you are using,

00:02:48.260 --> 00:02:50.792
you won't be able to find
out how to play the game,

00:02:50.792 --> 00:02:52.000
how to be in the chess board.

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:52.880
Simon Wardley's here.

00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:55.330
He's doing a talk about
much more of this later.

00:02:55.330 --> 00:02:58.880
But we used these techniques
to identify where we were.

00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:02.840
The other thing is
measure where you are.

00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:04.632
Now, who here thinks
the government is big?

00:03:04.632 --> 00:03:05.840
The British-- think about it.

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:06.890
The British government.

00:03:06.890 --> 00:03:09.860
Would you say it was a big
organization, 400,000 people,

00:03:09.860 --> 00:03:13.040
lots of things,
aircraft carriers?

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:14.960
We have huge buildings.

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:18.280
Government itself, it
turns out, isn't very big.

00:03:18.280 --> 00:03:21.770
The compute load to run a
government isn't enormous.

00:03:21.770 --> 00:03:25.610
Government itself is, in fact,
not large in compute terms.

00:03:25.610 --> 00:03:27.500
It's hell of a complex.

00:03:27.500 --> 00:03:28.940
But it's not large.

00:03:28.940 --> 00:03:30.230
And so we use some tools.

00:03:30.230 --> 00:03:33.100
Just using transparency
of data helped

00:03:33.100 --> 00:03:36.320
to identify what we
were doing and also

00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:39.260
how much we were doing.

00:03:39.260 --> 00:03:42.650
So the initial change, we
had to bring in controls.

00:03:42.650 --> 00:03:44.780
We brought in the process
of spend controls.

00:03:44.780 --> 00:03:47.001
And those spend controls
helped us identify

00:03:47.001 --> 00:03:49.250
whether the people were
building things that we really

00:03:49.250 --> 00:03:50.806
needed at that point.

00:03:50.806 --> 00:03:51.430
Think about it.

00:03:51.430 --> 00:03:54.930
2011, we were still at the
end of a financial crisis.

00:03:54.930 --> 00:03:57.980
It was very important that we
only started building things

00:03:57.980 --> 00:04:00.920
that we needed to use that
we hadn't built elsewhere.

00:04:00.920 --> 00:04:03.650
And that meant you had to have
a view over the whole system,

00:04:03.650 --> 00:04:06.080
over the whole ecosystem
within a government,

00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:08.870
to check why you were building
one thing in one place

00:04:08.870 --> 00:04:10.730
and one thing in another.

00:04:10.730 --> 00:04:13.680
Now, if you look at
how technology grows,

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:16.730
you'll see that it starts
from things that are very new,

00:04:16.730 --> 00:04:20.360
and they move through from
ubiquity through transition

00:04:20.360 --> 00:04:24.770
through to a common or
a commodity service.

00:04:24.770 --> 00:04:27.260
Now, we realized
that as a government,

00:04:27.260 --> 00:04:30.890
this speed of transition of
technology is much, much faster

00:04:30.890 --> 00:04:33.110
than we could react to.

00:04:33.110 --> 00:04:36.560
So our game was not about
being a part of that cycle.

00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:38.990
Our game was trying
to identify how

00:04:38.990 --> 00:04:41.900
we worked within that cycle.

00:04:41.900 --> 00:04:44.360
The problem was,
we had done this.

00:04:44.360 --> 00:04:47.300
All of the things that we
were using were special.

00:04:47.300 --> 00:04:50.000
Everything that we made,
everything we created,

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:54.140
everything that had been built
for us was special, and very

00:04:54.140 --> 00:04:58.010
much special to each
department, and sometimes

00:04:58.010 --> 00:05:02.340
special within a department, to
a particular business process.

00:05:02.340 --> 00:05:04.130
Now, that's a
problem about the way

00:05:04.130 --> 00:05:05.780
that governments spend money.

00:05:05.780 --> 00:05:08.540
This is how the business
case process works.

00:05:08.540 --> 00:05:12.200
You get a business case through
to run an immigration system

00:05:12.200 --> 00:05:14.870
at a different time when you
get a business process through

00:05:14.870 --> 00:05:16.807
to run a visa system.

00:05:16.807 --> 00:05:18.890
But when you sit down and
think about it, the visa

00:05:18.890 --> 00:05:20.360
system and the
immigration system

00:05:20.360 --> 00:05:23.330
all use very similar components.

00:05:23.330 --> 00:05:25.100
We just hadn't
identified it, and had

00:05:25.100 --> 00:05:27.410
just gone and bought,
and gone and bought,

00:05:27.410 --> 00:05:28.550
and gone and bought.

00:05:28.550 --> 00:05:31.660
In many ways, we were procuring
our way out of trouble,

00:05:31.660 --> 00:05:33.070
or so we thought.

00:05:33.070 --> 00:05:36.410
But in fact, we were
digging a deeper hole,

00:05:36.410 --> 00:05:39.380
because what we'd done is
we then locked everything

00:05:39.380 --> 00:05:43.010
into a series of strong
system integrator contracts,

00:05:43.010 --> 00:05:46.130
which meant that we couldn't
break out of the chain

00:05:46.130 --> 00:05:49.730
and cause the change we needed,
because we had contractually

00:05:49.730 --> 00:05:53.420
agreed to do things
in one particular way.

00:05:53.420 --> 00:05:56.870
The things that we had
decided in 2008 to do

00:05:56.870 --> 00:06:00.590
were going to be
valid until 2017.

00:06:00.590 --> 00:06:03.270
Now, that's an
enormous, enormous jump

00:06:03.270 --> 00:06:04.970
for an organization to take.

00:06:04.970 --> 00:06:07.100
You've really got to
know that nothing's

00:06:07.100 --> 00:06:08.540
going to change
during that period

00:06:08.540 --> 00:06:10.490
if you're going to make
money in that space

00:06:10.490 --> 00:06:13.230
and run an efficient service.

00:06:13.230 --> 00:06:17.690
So first thing, we had
to change our shape.

00:06:17.690 --> 00:06:19.670
Governments are built like this.

00:06:19.670 --> 00:06:23.750
You have the Department of
International Development,

00:06:23.750 --> 00:06:26.960
the Department of Agriculture,
the Department of Transport,

00:06:26.960 --> 00:06:28.520
the Ministry of Education.

00:06:28.520 --> 00:06:30.680
They're all separate entities.

00:06:30.680 --> 00:06:32.480
And all of them,
for years, have been

00:06:32.480 --> 00:06:34.940
running as separate
entities, and they

00:06:34.940 --> 00:06:36.650
haven't been using this.

00:06:36.650 --> 00:06:38.840
They haven't been using
any form of approach

00:06:38.840 --> 00:06:41.420
to platform or
commonality, mostly

00:06:41.420 --> 00:06:43.760
because they didn't know what
the other departments were

00:06:43.760 --> 00:06:44.480
doing.

00:06:44.480 --> 00:06:48.500
Just the simple act of
centralizing and opening data

00:06:48.500 --> 00:06:52.370
enabled us to see how much
and how many things we

00:06:52.370 --> 00:06:55.440
were doing that were common.

00:06:55.440 --> 00:06:57.870
We also found there were
four main obstacles.

00:06:57.870 --> 00:07:00.564
Now, I refer to this as
the square of despair,

00:07:00.564 --> 00:07:02.480
because if you take over
a large organization,

00:07:02.480 --> 00:07:04.688
one of the problems you're
going to find straightaway

00:07:04.688 --> 00:07:07.190
is these four
things will get you.

00:07:07.190 --> 00:07:11.150
OK, now, the most pernicious
of these I'll save for last.

00:07:11.150 --> 00:07:12.950
But, capability.

00:07:12.950 --> 00:07:15.350
We had to find
modern technologists

00:07:15.350 --> 00:07:17.420
to come and work
with us, and that

00:07:17.420 --> 00:07:20.280
meant we needed to offer people
great opportunities to come

00:07:20.280 --> 00:07:21.740
and work in a government.

00:07:21.740 --> 00:07:23.570
And many times, working
for the government

00:07:23.570 --> 00:07:26.300
doesn't appear to be
an attractive thing.

00:07:26.300 --> 00:07:29.000
I've got lots of friends
who do very boring jobs

00:07:29.000 --> 00:07:32.240
and get paid lots of
money for doing that.

00:07:32.240 --> 00:07:36.230
I offer completely
the opposite of that.

00:07:36.230 --> 00:07:38.150
In government, you
have the opportunity

00:07:38.150 --> 00:07:42.260
to change your government,
change what your nation does,

00:07:42.260 --> 00:07:45.840
but we never pay government
at the same rate.

00:07:45.840 --> 00:07:47.880
And part of that is
because it is a sort

00:07:47.880 --> 00:07:49.260
of trade-off at that point.

00:07:49.260 --> 00:07:50.820
The experience
that you will gain

00:07:50.820 --> 00:07:53.550
in three years in
government is like 10 years

00:07:53.550 --> 00:07:56.910
in another organization, because
if you know what you're doing,

00:07:56.910 --> 00:07:59.790
your capacity and your
capability to change things

00:07:59.790 --> 00:08:01.380
is immense.

00:08:01.380 --> 00:08:04.380
And that's what attracted
200 great technology leaders

00:08:04.380 --> 00:08:07.210
to come back and
work in government.

00:08:07.210 --> 00:08:09.120
Next thing is security.

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:11.100
We were still
running our security

00:08:11.100 --> 00:08:14.670
with a view to being in
the Cold War, really.

00:08:14.670 --> 00:08:19.260
We had officials who were
working on social impact bonds

00:08:19.260 --> 00:08:22.260
and charities under the
same security restrictions

00:08:22.260 --> 00:08:25.510
as people working in an
intelligence organization.

00:08:25.510 --> 00:08:27.010
So we had to move.

00:08:27.010 --> 00:08:29.640
Most of government and the
delivery of public services,

00:08:29.640 --> 00:08:33.990
97% of a government and the
delivery of public services,

00:08:33.990 --> 00:08:38.520
is able to run at the same
level of security as a Skadden

00:08:38.520 --> 00:08:41.100
or a Pricewaterhouse
or a Deloitte.

00:08:41.100 --> 00:08:43.380
It's a great professional
services firm.

00:08:43.380 --> 00:08:45.570
So we needed to
run at that rate,

00:08:45.570 --> 00:08:48.210
and that meant we had to review
all of our security settings

00:08:48.210 --> 00:08:50.940
and introduce one
common security layer

00:08:50.940 --> 00:08:53.160
called Official,
at which everything

00:08:53.160 --> 00:08:56.730
can work that is not
national security-related.

00:08:56.730 --> 00:08:59.880
That also meant that we stopped
spending huge amounts of money

00:08:59.880 --> 00:09:04.200
securing and locking down
people that needed to be open,

00:09:04.200 --> 00:09:08.610
and we could spend the money
on the secure areas properly.

00:09:08.610 --> 00:09:09.980
Then there's procurement.

00:09:09.980 --> 00:09:13.140
Now, procurement is very
difficult sometimes to break,

00:09:13.140 --> 00:09:15.630
because it's the thing
that's always there.

00:09:15.630 --> 00:09:17.970
It's the thing which all
governments always run with.

00:09:17.970 --> 00:09:20.869
All large organizations have
issues with procurement.

00:09:20.869 --> 00:09:22.410
And we had to make
sure that we could

00:09:22.410 --> 00:09:25.030
start procuring not
just from big companies,

00:09:25.030 --> 00:09:27.160
but from the best companies.

00:09:27.160 --> 00:09:30.040
And a little bit later I'll
explain how we did that.

00:09:30.040 --> 00:09:32.280
But the best was what
we were looking for,

00:09:32.280 --> 00:09:34.650
not the biggest,
not the people that

00:09:34.650 --> 00:09:37.740
could spend 60,000 pounds as
if it were the drop of a hat

00:09:37.740 --> 00:09:40.350
to fill in a procurement form
and run a bid team for three

00:09:40.350 --> 00:09:41.310
months.

00:09:41.310 --> 00:09:42.930
We needed to be
able to run things

00:09:42.930 --> 00:09:46.680
where we could buy from the
best in the market, the most

00:09:46.680 --> 00:09:47.830
innovative.

00:09:47.830 --> 00:09:49.950
And many times, what we
were doing in procurement

00:09:49.950 --> 00:09:51.990
was putting those people off.

00:09:51.990 --> 00:09:54.300
And then legacy, the
most pernicious of all.

00:09:54.300 --> 00:09:56.490
You can't do that,
because it's legacy.

00:09:56.490 --> 00:09:58.680
You can't change that,
because if you change it,

00:09:58.680 --> 00:09:59.400
you'll break it.

00:09:59.400 --> 00:10:01.950
There will be riots
in the streets.

00:10:01.950 --> 00:10:04.680
I had that said to
me several times.

00:10:04.680 --> 00:10:06.870
We created a way of
moving legacy away.

00:10:06.870 --> 00:10:08.470
It's called the Crown
Hosting Service.

00:10:08.470 --> 00:10:10.500
What we do is we move
our legacy systems

00:10:10.500 --> 00:10:14.790
into a special hosted
service which we run.

00:10:14.790 --> 00:10:18.030
It's a zombie insurance
fund in the end.

00:10:18.030 --> 00:10:19.740
It allows you to put stuff in.

00:10:19.740 --> 00:10:21.200
The legacy can be looked after.

00:10:21.200 --> 00:10:24.330
It can all be helped to stay
there whilst you can then

00:10:24.330 --> 00:10:25.950
focus on the future.

00:10:25.950 --> 00:10:29.190
And your future is about
building things in the cloud.

00:10:29.190 --> 00:10:30.960
Now, culturally,
different organizations

00:10:30.960 --> 00:10:32.002
do things different ways.

00:10:32.002 --> 00:10:33.418
I'm not saying
that's the way that

00:10:33.418 --> 00:10:35.550
would work immediately
for you, but believe me,

00:10:35.550 --> 00:10:36.990
that works for us.

00:10:36.990 --> 00:10:39.780
The creation of our own Hotel
California for government

00:10:39.780 --> 00:10:43.650
IT has meant that we can put
stuff in, you can put it in,

00:10:43.650 --> 00:10:46.530
we'll run it, it's nice, it
will always be looked after.

00:10:46.530 --> 00:10:49.540
You just can never check it out.

00:10:49.540 --> 00:10:52.510
So we found that technology was
moving faster than government.

00:10:52.510 --> 00:10:54.550
We had to be free to keep up.

00:10:54.550 --> 00:10:56.495
We had to change
shape, as I said.

00:10:56.495 --> 00:10:57.370
So keep that in mind.

00:10:57.370 --> 00:10:58.780
How do you introduce platforms?

00:10:58.780 --> 00:11:01.900
How do you introduce common
services to an organization

00:11:01.900 --> 00:11:05.920
that is a large organization
full of tremendously

00:11:05.920 --> 00:11:09.910
gifted professional
people, but who have always

00:11:09.910 --> 00:11:13.040
been working in silos?

00:11:13.040 --> 00:11:15.130
So, the changes we made.

00:11:15.130 --> 00:11:18.546
First, we had to say
what good looked like.

00:11:18.546 --> 00:11:20.170
So we wrote down what
good looked like.

00:11:20.170 --> 00:11:22.960
We literally wrote
a manual for CTOs,

00:11:22.960 --> 00:11:25.960
which we then changed into the
technology code of practice.

00:11:25.960 --> 00:11:29.977
And this was agreed by everybody
who was a technology leader.

00:11:29.977 --> 00:11:31.810
So we convened everybody,
made sure everyone

00:11:31.810 --> 00:11:34.420
agreed what good looked like.

00:11:34.420 --> 00:11:36.100
We then agreed some
design principles,

00:11:36.100 --> 00:11:39.190
because if you're designing
services for citizens,

00:11:39.190 --> 00:11:40.870
we want to have a
common approach.

00:11:40.870 --> 00:11:43.090
A citizen does not
look at the government

00:11:43.090 --> 00:11:45.400
and think, actually,
that's really interesting,

00:11:45.400 --> 00:11:47.370
it's 24 different departments.

00:11:47.370 --> 00:11:49.390
A citizen looks at the
government and goes,

00:11:49.390 --> 00:11:50.560
it's the government.

00:11:50.560 --> 00:11:51.690
It's one thing.

00:11:51.690 --> 00:11:54.190
So if you're going to have a
common approach to how you deal

00:11:54.190 --> 00:11:57.490
with citizens, you need to
have common design principles,

00:11:57.490 --> 00:12:01.960
again, which are agreed
and which are shared.

00:12:01.960 --> 00:12:03.670
One of the things
we did to make sure

00:12:03.670 --> 00:12:05.470
that we could work
properly is we realized

00:12:05.470 --> 00:12:08.200
we were going to be using
the internet quite a lot,

00:12:08.200 --> 00:12:11.120
so we needed to
have open standards.

00:12:11.120 --> 00:12:14.950
And one of the most difficult
open standards we had to use

00:12:14.950 --> 00:12:16.540
was how do you
explain to a citizen

00:12:16.540 --> 00:12:18.340
that they need to,
at some points,

00:12:18.340 --> 00:12:22.210
have a document which they need
to fill in or change or modify?

00:12:22.210 --> 00:12:24.910
We had to do that in a way
that didn't require people

00:12:24.910 --> 00:12:27.950
to sign in and use one
particular piece of software.

00:12:27.950 --> 00:12:30.310
So we defined what an
open document format

00:12:30.310 --> 00:12:31.902
should be for the government.

00:12:31.902 --> 00:12:33.610
There was enormous
amount of peer review.

00:12:33.610 --> 00:12:35.410
We did the same
consultation process

00:12:35.410 --> 00:12:37.570
as you would do for
opening a new airport

00:12:37.570 --> 00:12:39.280
to make sure we could do that.

00:12:39.280 --> 00:12:41.260
But that brought
in the capability

00:12:41.260 --> 00:12:44.120
to have common open standards.

00:12:44.120 --> 00:12:47.220
And again, just culturally,
this held people's views

00:12:47.220 --> 00:12:50.200
that open standards
are important.

00:12:50.200 --> 00:12:51.800
We cut out a lot of friction.

00:12:51.800 --> 00:12:53.500
So that was the
organization chart

00:12:53.500 --> 00:12:55.450
that I had when I started.

00:12:55.450 --> 00:12:58.810
If any of you can work
out how to do this bit,

00:12:58.810 --> 00:12:59.570
I never got there.

00:12:59.570 --> 00:13:01.870
I never worked out
that bit on the right,

00:13:01.870 --> 00:13:03.819
because it was just too complex.

00:13:03.819 --> 00:13:04.610
So we changed that.

00:13:04.610 --> 00:13:05.509
We introduced this.

00:13:05.509 --> 00:13:06.550
This is our organization.

00:13:06.550 --> 00:13:08.440
This is our primary motivation.

00:13:08.440 --> 00:13:11.860
Our primary organizing
thing was a browser.

00:13:11.860 --> 00:13:14.680
And if you use a browser, these
things become really simple.

00:13:14.680 --> 00:13:17.020
You can identify where you
have common services that you

00:13:17.020 --> 00:13:17.950
all share.

00:13:17.950 --> 00:13:20.590
You can identify where you
have common specific things

00:13:20.590 --> 00:13:25.180
like ERP, which just should
be done in a similar way.

00:13:25.180 --> 00:13:28.600
And then it allows you to create
new, great, digital services,

00:13:28.600 --> 00:13:31.750
allows you to create the
mission IT, the internal IT

00:13:31.750 --> 00:13:34.810
to work with people,
taking away and abstracting

00:13:34.810 --> 00:13:39.340
all of that gumpf which
is the infrastructure.

00:13:39.340 --> 00:13:42.550
And using the cloud for
that, and declaring the cloud

00:13:42.550 --> 00:13:45.670
as the primary place for
that is the main change

00:13:45.670 --> 00:13:47.060
within that space.

00:13:47.060 --> 00:13:49.960
And we needed to work with
lots of innovative companies.

00:13:49.960 --> 00:13:53.110
So we changed the way
that we worked with people

00:13:53.110 --> 00:13:53.910
in our marketplace.

00:13:53.910 --> 00:13:59.020
We created a new tech
sector called GovTech.

00:13:59.020 --> 00:14:01.690
And this GovTech
sector was about,

00:14:01.690 --> 00:14:07.310
how do we bring people in
to work with the government

00:14:07.310 --> 00:14:10.540
and make it easy for them
to work with the government?

00:14:10.540 --> 00:14:13.390
Not make it easy for
government to work with them.

00:14:13.390 --> 00:14:15.490
It's a really
important difference.

00:14:15.490 --> 00:14:17.200
And we grew a huge
number of jobs.

00:14:17.200 --> 00:14:19.600
We drew a huge number
of innovations.

00:14:19.600 --> 00:14:24.100
But what happened was this meant
that our tech cost less and was

00:14:24.100 --> 00:14:28.420
better because we were going
to companies that specialized

00:14:28.420 --> 00:14:32.170
in particular components, rather
than going to one big company

00:14:32.170 --> 00:14:36.280
and saying, please take it
all away from me and do it.

00:14:36.280 --> 00:14:38.830
So that's 1.6 billion sales.

00:14:38.830 --> 00:14:40.420
55-- 55.

00:14:40.420 --> 00:14:41.430
I've got a new number.

00:14:41.430 --> 00:14:42.520
It was 54.

00:14:42.520 --> 00:14:45.940
55% of that is
small-medium enterprises.

00:14:45.940 --> 00:14:48.280
So it's a way of government
helping build the innovation

00:14:48.280 --> 00:14:50.350
and helping grow the businesses.

00:14:50.350 --> 00:14:53.100
And so we now feel the UK is
a center of digital excellence

00:14:53.100 --> 00:14:53.949
in government.

00:14:53.949 --> 00:14:55.990
We're the number one
digital government according

00:14:55.990 --> 00:14:59.950
to the United Nations, and
we have started our move

00:14:59.950 --> 00:15:02.110
because what we've done
is instead of treating

00:15:02.110 --> 00:15:04.780
our technology on the
left of that diagram,

00:15:04.780 --> 00:15:07.810
we're now doing so much
more of it on the right.

00:15:07.810 --> 00:15:12.370
We're doing so much more using
the capability of common tools

00:15:12.370 --> 00:15:14.320
so that you can focus
on the things that

00:15:14.320 --> 00:15:15.760
are important in
government, which

00:15:15.760 --> 00:15:18.920
is building great services
for your citizens.

00:15:18.920 --> 00:15:22.390
Now, a lot of this
is down to the use

00:15:22.390 --> 00:15:25.400
of one four-letter word, Open.

00:15:25.400 --> 00:15:27.100
Open helped us change this.

00:15:27.100 --> 00:15:28.360
Open was our disruptor.

00:15:28.360 --> 00:15:30.190
If you need to
construct a narrative

00:15:30.190 --> 00:15:34.240
to change your
government, Open works.

00:15:34.240 --> 00:15:36.250
So four things we
did that were open.

00:15:36.250 --> 00:15:37.480
One, open markets.

00:15:37.480 --> 00:15:40.570
As you'll see, we had a
small number of providers.

00:15:40.570 --> 00:15:42.190
We now have lots of providers.

00:15:42.190 --> 00:15:45.220
We have an open market which
people will come and invest in,

00:15:45.220 --> 00:15:49.950
and people will grow their
businesses in in the UK.

00:15:49.950 --> 00:15:51.910
We committed to using the cloud.

00:15:51.910 --> 00:15:55.630
We committed to using the
internet to run our government.

00:15:55.630 --> 00:15:58.630
And by doing that, that meant
that we had to-- because it's

00:15:58.630 --> 00:16:00.970
a common service, it's
a common resource,

00:16:00.970 --> 00:16:03.730
we have to commit to
using open standards.

00:16:03.730 --> 00:16:05.650
We have to be very
adult about the fact

00:16:05.650 --> 00:16:08.440
that we want to use open
standards across the whole

00:16:08.440 --> 00:16:09.560
of the government.

00:16:09.560 --> 00:16:13.440
So if you are a police force,
or you are an ambulance service,

00:16:13.440 --> 00:16:16.240
or you're a fire
and rescue service,

00:16:16.240 --> 00:16:20.830
you should be getting
your updates and your data

00:16:20.830 --> 00:16:25.690
about incidents in a common
format, in a common way,

00:16:25.690 --> 00:16:28.540
according to common standards,
so you can all work together.

00:16:28.540 --> 00:16:31.770
It actually starts to get
really important at that point

00:16:31.770 --> 00:16:34.061
that people can understand
what's going on.

00:16:34.061 --> 00:16:35.560
And that was one
of the main lessons

00:16:35.560 --> 00:16:37.660
we learned about the bombings--

00:16:37.660 --> 00:16:41.050
the 7/7 bombings, was that
all of our emergency services

00:16:41.050 --> 00:16:44.290
had to work together,
clearly, effectively.

00:16:44.290 --> 00:16:46.120
And open standards
are right at the core

00:16:46.120 --> 00:16:48.970
not of that, but across the
whole approach to what we

00:16:48.970 --> 00:16:53.320
do as government, so that we can
use the resources effectively.

00:16:53.320 --> 00:16:54.690
We committed to open data.

00:16:54.690 --> 00:16:57.400
You'll know that Tim Berners-Lee
and Nigel Shadbolt established

00:16:57.400 --> 00:16:59.170
the Open Data Institute.

00:16:59.170 --> 00:17:03.340
And we started to publish
data about what we were doing,

00:17:03.340 --> 00:17:05.869
not just about spending.

00:17:05.869 --> 00:17:07.420
I actually passed
a legislation that

00:17:07.420 --> 00:17:09.460
meant that local
authorities would

00:17:09.460 --> 00:17:12.400
publish all of their
spending over 500 pounds.

00:17:12.400 --> 00:17:14.349
We then started
introducing new tools

00:17:14.349 --> 00:17:17.296
to help people change their
behavior using open data.

00:17:17.296 --> 00:17:18.670
One of the most
successful things

00:17:18.670 --> 00:17:22.000
we did in a local government
was we published the energy

00:17:22.000 --> 00:17:24.200
usage of our buildings.

00:17:24.200 --> 00:17:26.829
By publishing the energy
usage of the buildings

00:17:26.829 --> 00:17:29.050
then meant that everyone
could see which building

00:17:29.050 --> 00:17:31.677
was leaving the lights on.

00:17:31.677 --> 00:17:34.010
And actually, by putting the
email address of the person

00:17:34.010 --> 00:17:35.770
responsible for the
building, we managed

00:17:35.770 --> 00:17:41.000
to save 25% of our energy
spend without actually

00:17:41.000 --> 00:17:42.050
talking to anybody.

00:17:42.050 --> 00:17:45.330
It just happened, because
people wrote in and went,

00:17:45.330 --> 00:17:46.859
you've left the lights on.

00:17:46.859 --> 00:17:48.650
And as soon as that
happened, the officials

00:17:48.650 --> 00:17:49.550
changed the behavior.

00:17:49.550 --> 00:17:50.360
It was wonderful.

00:17:50.360 --> 00:17:53.720
It was a really good way
of making data work for us

00:17:53.720 --> 00:17:55.230
and use open data.

00:17:55.230 --> 00:17:58.430
But also, the open data that we
publish around our government

00:17:58.430 --> 00:18:01.280
helps now power big
companies I don't

00:18:01.280 --> 00:18:04.100
know if you saw on stage during
that keynote a guy called

00:18:04.100 --> 00:18:06.400
Herman Narula from Improbable.

00:18:06.400 --> 00:18:08.750
But Improbable
runs on state data.

00:18:08.750 --> 00:18:11.120
The city they've constructed
is constructed out

00:18:11.120 --> 00:18:13.850
of the open data that we
provide as a government.

00:18:13.850 --> 00:18:17.600
We have, in our hands, an
enormous amount of the data

00:18:17.600 --> 00:18:22.430
that we can provide and run
and help our economy with,

00:18:22.430 --> 00:18:24.650
and yet we didn't make
it open in the past.

00:18:24.650 --> 00:18:26.690
We can really help that.

00:18:26.690 --> 00:18:28.634
And then finally, open source.

00:18:28.634 --> 00:18:30.800
I'll talk a little bit more
about why open source is

00:18:30.800 --> 00:18:35.420
so important to us, but open
source was one of those four

00:18:35.420 --> 00:18:39.360
that drove the change of
what we did about government.

00:18:39.360 --> 00:18:42.110
And it's the heart of what
other governments are doing.

00:18:42.110 --> 00:18:44.570
If you look at those
four governments,

00:18:44.570 --> 00:18:48.830
you'll notice that they
are similar to us in a way.

00:18:48.830 --> 00:18:50.270
We picked to work with these.

00:18:50.270 --> 00:18:52.535
We formed a group called
the D5, because we

00:18:52.535 --> 00:18:55.400
were all at the similar
stages of our growth.

00:18:55.400 --> 00:18:58.340
The Korean government
on the bottom right

00:18:58.340 --> 00:19:01.830
started to build a digital
marketplace rather like our G

00:19:01.830 --> 00:19:03.620
Cloud, like our
digital marketplace,

00:19:03.620 --> 00:19:06.320
about four years before
we did, because they

00:19:06.320 --> 00:19:09.290
identified that they needed
to get that innovation in.

00:19:09.290 --> 00:19:12.460
The Estonian government,
possibly-- and everybody

00:19:12.460 --> 00:19:15.120
always cites the Estonians as
a great digital government--

00:19:15.120 --> 00:19:17.390
use a component called the
X-Road, which they've open

00:19:17.390 --> 00:19:18.980
sourced, which is
a component which

00:19:18.980 --> 00:19:22.220
allows government services
to work together so that you

00:19:22.220 --> 00:19:22.970
can join up.

00:19:22.970 --> 00:19:26.960
It's a core part of one of
their great signature policies,

00:19:26.960 --> 00:19:29.120
which is called Once Only.

00:19:29.120 --> 00:19:32.090
Once you've told the government
who you are and where you live,

00:19:32.090 --> 00:19:36.000
the government should never
be required to ask you again.

00:19:36.000 --> 00:19:37.790
That's wonderfully simple.

00:19:37.790 --> 00:19:39.609
But if you imagine
the mess that you

00:19:39.609 --> 00:19:41.150
get when you start
a government, it's

00:19:41.150 --> 00:19:42.680
quite difficult to get there.

00:19:42.680 --> 00:19:44.000
But that's where you go.

00:19:44.000 --> 00:19:46.490
That's what they've got.

00:19:46.490 --> 00:19:48.380
New Zealand has
done a lot of work.

00:19:48.380 --> 00:19:52.430
They did great pioneering
work on identity with RealMe.

00:19:52.430 --> 00:19:55.490
And also, I remember first time
I walked into a New Zealand

00:19:55.490 --> 00:19:58.430
government office and there
were storyboards and profiles

00:19:58.430 --> 00:20:02.810
and people working in a
proper way, proper agile way,

00:20:02.810 --> 00:20:05.180
on new developments.

00:20:05.180 --> 00:20:07.950
And we realized that New
Zealand was very like the UK

00:20:07.950 --> 00:20:11.540
in where we were in our
journey, and as was Israel.

00:20:11.540 --> 00:20:14.752
And you'll notice that
gov.il, on the top left,

00:20:14.752 --> 00:20:16.460
they've taken off the
picture these days,

00:20:16.460 --> 00:20:19.670
but gov.il and the New
Zealand government site

00:20:19.670 --> 00:20:23.360
looks rather like
gov.uk, and there's

00:20:23.360 --> 00:20:26.210
a reason for that,
because to a large part,

00:20:26.210 --> 00:20:28.870
they are the same thing.

00:20:28.870 --> 00:20:30.837
So we created the same thing.

00:20:30.837 --> 00:20:33.420
We created the government of the
internet, a government that's

00:20:33.420 --> 00:20:35.380
started using the
internet properly

00:20:35.380 --> 00:20:38.210
and effectively to
deliver its services.

00:20:38.210 --> 00:20:40.450
Let's just have a think
about what's next.

00:20:40.450 --> 00:20:42.280
We used Open to unlock change.

00:20:42.280 --> 00:20:46.690
We used Open to create
competition to help us grow.

00:20:46.690 --> 00:20:48.730
Competition was the
thing that drove it.

00:20:48.730 --> 00:20:50.500
It wasn't about, say,
we didn't stand up

00:20:50.500 --> 00:20:52.600
and say, everything
must be open source.

00:20:52.600 --> 00:20:55.810
What we did was we created
the conditions for success

00:20:55.810 --> 00:20:59.650
for people that worked
using open standards.

00:20:59.650 --> 00:21:02.440
And in doing so, open
source became more and more

00:21:02.440 --> 00:21:04.960
a component of our government.

00:21:04.960 --> 00:21:08.950
In 2011, I was sat down by a
security analyst who read out

00:21:08.950 --> 00:21:13.600
why you should not use open
source software to me, in front

00:21:13.600 --> 00:21:15.670
of his team, so
that he felt that he

00:21:15.670 --> 00:21:17.980
had delivered the lesson to me.

00:21:17.980 --> 00:21:19.690
It was one of those
things that I always

00:21:19.690 --> 00:21:23.172
remember, because he's
not saying that now.

00:21:23.172 --> 00:21:25.380
It's really obvious that
that was a huge [INAUDIBLE].

00:21:25.380 --> 00:21:27.780
But it wasn't that we'd said,
we will now back open source.

00:21:27.780 --> 00:21:29.446
It was we said we
will back competition.

00:21:29.446 --> 00:21:31.449
We will back openness.

00:21:31.449 --> 00:21:33.490
And also, the high level
of small-medium business

00:21:33.490 --> 00:21:36.220
engagement is there because
we back competition,

00:21:36.220 --> 00:21:39.010
that we back the ability
for a small company

00:21:39.010 --> 00:21:43.450
to do exactly the same
work as a large company,

00:21:43.450 --> 00:21:46.210
and possibly at a lower rate.

00:21:46.210 --> 00:21:48.430
Some of the times, when
we started to get going--

00:21:48.430 --> 00:21:50.880
famously there was
an occasion when

00:21:50.880 --> 00:21:54.160
a hosting service was being
sold by a company for 5

00:21:54.160 --> 00:21:54.850
million pounds.

00:21:54.850 --> 00:21:59.330
We got exactly the
same thing for 54,000.

00:21:59.330 --> 00:22:03.080
Two factors, two
factors of difference,

00:22:03.080 --> 00:22:06.360
two orders of
magnitude difference.

00:22:06.360 --> 00:22:08.200
And so we moved from
one to the other.

00:22:08.200 --> 00:22:09.220
We moved from silos.

00:22:09.220 --> 00:22:11.944
We introduced common
platform services.

00:22:11.944 --> 00:22:13.360
And those common
platform services

00:22:13.360 --> 00:22:15.850
allowed us to rationalize
what we were doing

00:22:15.850 --> 00:22:18.130
and also allowed us to
share what people are doing.

00:22:18.130 --> 00:22:23.440
And services like gov UK verify
and gov UK pay and gov UK

00:22:23.440 --> 00:22:25.840
notify are platform
services that

00:22:25.840 --> 00:22:30.580
enable you to deliver common
functionality to parts

00:22:30.580 --> 00:22:31.750
of government.

00:22:31.750 --> 00:22:34.620
Think for a second about Notify.

00:22:34.620 --> 00:22:37.930
So Notify, which is run by one
of my heroes in government,

00:22:37.930 --> 00:22:44.840
a guy called Pete Hurley,
Notify shows you where

00:22:44.840 --> 00:22:46.760
and tells you when your tools--

00:22:46.760 --> 00:22:48.670
your passport, your
driving license--

00:22:48.670 --> 00:22:50.990
when it is coming back.

00:22:50.990 --> 00:22:54.010
Now, we get half a billion
telephone calls a year.

00:22:54.010 --> 00:22:57.072
25% of them were to
say, I don't know where

00:22:57.072 --> 00:22:58.280
something is on the internet.

00:22:58.280 --> 00:22:59.160
Can you help?

00:22:59.160 --> 00:23:00.740
In your website,
how do I find it?

00:23:00.740 --> 00:23:04.310
So we made a website
that worked for the user,

00:23:04.310 --> 00:23:05.469
and it generated savings.

00:23:05.469 --> 00:23:07.010
Those savings, if
you think about it,

00:23:07.010 --> 00:23:09.210
were people not calling.

00:23:09.210 --> 00:23:11.630
And if you're getting half
a billion telephone calls

00:23:11.630 --> 00:23:16.070
at 12 pounds a call, there's
a lot of money to save.

00:23:16.070 --> 00:23:19.040
25% of those were people
saying, where's my thing?

00:23:19.040 --> 00:23:21.650
So by introducing a
notification service,

00:23:21.650 --> 00:23:24.140
you can help people find out
where their services are,

00:23:24.140 --> 00:23:27.200
find out where their passport,
their driving license,

00:23:27.200 --> 00:23:30.080
or their application
for a license is.

00:23:30.080 --> 00:23:34.262
And people can then not
call in, and we save money.

00:23:34.262 --> 00:23:36.470
And all of these things are
sensible, straightforward

00:23:36.470 --> 00:23:39.740
approaches to government which
we've started to bring in.

00:23:39.740 --> 00:23:42.410
But I wanted to, as we talk
about Open and open source,

00:23:42.410 --> 00:23:44.840
just have a think about
the picture of governments.

00:23:44.840 --> 00:23:46.820
There's lots of people
from different countries

00:23:46.820 --> 00:23:48.950
in this room, and you
all have a government.

00:23:48.950 --> 00:23:52.790
On the right hand side there is
the Norwegian driving license

00:23:52.790 --> 00:23:56.480
agency, or the Norwegian
Ministry of Transport.

00:23:56.480 --> 00:23:58.580
And they issue driving
licenses to Norwegians.

00:23:58.580 --> 00:24:00.260
They ask people
for their identity.

00:24:00.260 --> 00:24:01.580
They ask them for a photograph.

00:24:01.580 --> 00:24:02.230
They record it.

00:24:02.230 --> 00:24:03.950
They keep it in a database.

00:24:03.950 --> 00:24:06.650
It's really strange,
because the British DVLA

00:24:06.650 --> 00:24:08.405
do exactly the same thing.

00:24:11.320 --> 00:24:16.230
So why are we all trying to
do exactly the same thing

00:24:16.230 --> 00:24:17.310
differently?

00:24:17.310 --> 00:24:19.320
In America, there are 50 states.

00:24:19.320 --> 00:24:21.470
Each state issues
a driving license.

00:24:21.470 --> 00:24:23.940
They do exactly the
same thing, but issuing

00:24:23.940 --> 00:24:28.190
a driving license
in the UK is just

00:24:28.190 --> 00:24:30.030
a weird jurisdictional thing.

00:24:30.030 --> 00:24:32.310
We have a monopoly market.

00:24:32.310 --> 00:24:35.040
We don't compete.

00:24:35.040 --> 00:24:38.730
It may be good
news, but we're not

00:24:38.730 --> 00:24:42.150
going to set up a driver
license service in Norway,

00:24:42.150 --> 00:24:44.010
because they've already
got one, and they've

00:24:44.010 --> 00:24:45.900
got a monopoly on that market.

00:24:45.900 --> 00:24:47.900
But we do the same thing.

00:24:47.900 --> 00:24:50.790
And in compute terms, we're
doing exactly the same thing

00:24:50.790 --> 00:24:52.480
as we go through this.

00:24:52.480 --> 00:24:54.600
So this leads us to
think, as we learned

00:24:54.600 --> 00:24:58.020
by sharing the code of gov
UK, that actually, there's

00:24:58.020 --> 00:25:00.360
an opportunity to
share a lot more

00:25:00.360 --> 00:25:03.630
between different governments,
and that by sharing

00:25:03.630 --> 00:25:06.390
not just our code,
but our tooling

00:25:06.390 --> 00:25:09.360
and also the methodology
that we brought in,

00:25:09.360 --> 00:25:13.350
we can go and work and help
other governments to get over

00:25:13.350 --> 00:25:16.050
all the barriers that
we started to face,

00:25:16.050 --> 00:25:18.210
but in addition, and
one of the things

00:25:18.210 --> 00:25:20.280
we learned by sharing
with other governments,

00:25:20.280 --> 00:25:23.790
is that we get a hell of a lot
back in terms of knowledge,

00:25:23.790 --> 00:25:28.140
capability, and
help, because they've

00:25:28.140 --> 00:25:32.070
thought about these things in
a completely different way.

00:25:32.070 --> 00:25:35.090
So if you think about how
you would run and build

00:25:35.090 --> 00:25:37.980
a government, and
if you had turned up

00:25:37.980 --> 00:25:41.790
and found yourself needing to
work with a new government,

00:25:41.790 --> 00:25:45.060
one of the things you need to
do is work with open source

00:25:45.060 --> 00:25:45.990
properly.

00:25:45.990 --> 00:25:49.020
Those components of open
data and open standards

00:25:49.020 --> 00:25:53.230
play into this because as
you build your new services,

00:25:53.230 --> 00:25:56.930
and as you build your new
government in the cloud,

00:25:56.930 --> 00:25:57.840
open source can help.

00:25:57.840 --> 00:26:02.050
Let me just talk you through
this rather busy slide.

00:26:02.050 --> 00:26:04.754
Lots of people worry about
platform as a service,

00:26:04.754 --> 00:26:06.670
and worry about the
infrastructure underneath.

00:26:06.670 --> 00:26:09.580
And remember, most of
IT for the last 20 years

00:26:09.580 --> 00:26:12.940
has been about people building
great big infrastructures

00:26:12.940 --> 00:26:16.270
that they own and they can
go and polish every Tuesday

00:26:16.270 --> 00:26:17.400
when they need to.

00:26:17.400 --> 00:26:19.300
And they feel massively
secure because they

00:26:19.300 --> 00:26:20.950
can see the servers.

00:26:20.950 --> 00:26:22.480
When I first
started in politics,

00:26:22.480 --> 00:26:25.090
I walked into the local
government office, the data

00:26:25.090 --> 00:26:28.630
room of my local government,
and there were 130 servers

00:26:28.630 --> 00:26:31.570
and 130 cathode ray tubes.

00:26:31.570 --> 00:26:34.210
And it was 2007.

00:26:34.210 --> 00:26:37.720
It was like going
back in time then.

00:26:37.720 --> 00:26:39.580
Now, in three years,
we had made it

00:26:39.580 --> 00:26:41.230
a rule within the authority--

00:26:41.230 --> 00:26:45.430
2010, we bought our
last application server.

00:26:45.430 --> 00:26:47.920
We wrote in the Constitution
that the council would never

00:26:47.920 --> 00:26:50.890
buy another application server.

00:26:50.890 --> 00:26:52.930
In my world, if I
ran the world, it

00:26:52.930 --> 00:26:57.310
would be a criminal offense
to go and buy an email server.

00:26:57.310 --> 00:26:58.810
All of those things,
they're common.

00:26:58.810 --> 00:27:01.437
And if you move on to having
an abstractive platform

00:27:01.437 --> 00:27:03.520
as a service approach, it
means that your platform

00:27:03.520 --> 00:27:06.730
and your common infrastructure
should be there.

00:27:06.730 --> 00:27:09.220
We shouldn't talk about CIOs.

00:27:09.220 --> 00:27:11.850
I mean, a CIO to me is a
chief infrastructure officer.

00:27:11.850 --> 00:27:13.690
It's the person that
runs all of that stuff

00:27:13.690 --> 00:27:18.180
so that you can build
great things on top of it.

00:27:18.180 --> 00:27:20.010
So once you've got
the PaaS, then you

00:27:20.010 --> 00:27:21.700
move on to your registers.

00:27:21.700 --> 00:27:23.700
Now, as a government, one
of the glorious things

00:27:23.700 --> 00:27:25.390
we have is huge numbers.

00:27:25.390 --> 00:27:28.800
Imagine 300 years of
building departments.

00:27:28.800 --> 00:27:31.190
Can you imagine how many
different data sets we have?

00:27:31.190 --> 00:27:33.360
We have huge numbers of
data sets all the way

00:27:33.360 --> 00:27:35.490
across every department.

00:27:35.490 --> 00:27:38.580
The personnel data sets, as we
now build our digital economy

00:27:38.580 --> 00:27:40.770
bill, we have to create
a register of where

00:27:40.770 --> 00:27:43.020
we store data for people.

00:27:43.020 --> 00:27:45.720
We also need to create
registers of information.

00:27:45.720 --> 00:27:48.720
We found that we had
15 different lists

00:27:48.720 --> 00:27:51.585
of, and databases of, countries.

00:27:51.585 --> 00:27:52.210
Think about it.

00:27:52.210 --> 00:27:55.110
Even that most basic thing,
the names of another country,

00:27:55.110 --> 00:27:57.814
there were 15,
because everyone had

00:27:57.814 --> 00:27:59.730
an argument about whether
something was called

00:27:59.730 --> 00:28:01.800
the republic of this or that.

00:28:01.800 --> 00:28:04.820
And ultimately, if we're
going to work as a government,

00:28:04.820 --> 00:28:07.880
we have to recognize there
must be a list of countries.

00:28:07.880 --> 00:28:10.070
There must be a list
of universities.

00:28:10.070 --> 00:28:11.870
There must be a list of schools.

00:28:11.870 --> 00:28:14.210
If we only have one
list, the efficiency

00:28:14.210 --> 00:28:16.860
of running a government
becomes much more effective,

00:28:16.860 --> 00:28:19.615
but also things work more
effectively for people.

00:28:19.615 --> 00:28:20.990
We don't want to
get to the stage

00:28:20.990 --> 00:28:23.120
where you send somebody
to the wrong school,

00:28:23.120 --> 00:28:27.110
or you put people, or
pay the wrong university.

00:28:27.110 --> 00:28:29.522
And that can happen if
you have different lists.

00:28:29.522 --> 00:28:30.980
So we want to make
sure that works,

00:28:30.980 --> 00:28:33.650
and we have a program of
registers which is run by a guy

00:28:33.650 --> 00:28:36.410
called Paul Downey,
who is putting together

00:28:36.410 --> 00:28:39.800
this way of consolidating our
registers together so that you

00:28:39.800 --> 00:28:43.070
have a common approach
and a common set,

00:28:43.070 --> 00:28:46.640
a single canonical source,
persistent canonical source

00:28:46.640 --> 00:28:52.430
of data with provenance
and integrity.

00:28:52.430 --> 00:28:56.450
That allows you to build a
modern, functioning state.

00:28:56.450 --> 00:28:58.130
And then also open
source components,

00:28:58.130 --> 00:29:00.420
because we are
building components

00:29:00.420 --> 00:29:02.300
and we are building
our work in components.

00:29:02.300 --> 00:29:05.510
We can share those components
with other organizations.

00:29:05.510 --> 00:29:06.954
They can become better.

00:29:06.954 --> 00:29:09.120
And therefore, when we start
to build a new service,

00:29:09.120 --> 00:29:11.078
we don't have to build
everything from scratch.

00:29:11.078 --> 00:29:14.420
We can go and use the components
that people have made before.

00:29:14.420 --> 00:29:16.820
When they were building
the prison booking service,

00:29:16.820 --> 00:29:18.570
it was amazing because
people were saying,

00:29:18.570 --> 00:29:22.070
well, you know, the internet
solved booking years ago.

00:29:22.070 --> 00:29:25.400
Just work out the functionality
around this particular use

00:29:25.400 --> 00:29:27.620
case of doing it in prisons.

00:29:27.620 --> 00:29:29.390
And so by building
open source components

00:29:29.390 --> 00:29:30.860
with other
governments, we believe

00:29:30.860 --> 00:29:33.020
that we will move to a
much smoother and much more

00:29:33.020 --> 00:29:35.420
effective way of building
services that actually

00:29:35.420 --> 00:29:37.610
meet the needs of the users.

00:29:37.610 --> 00:29:39.320
And by applying
that discipline, it

00:29:39.320 --> 00:29:42.470
means that you can then
focus on building services

00:29:42.470 --> 00:29:45.770
that work for users,
that meet user needs.

00:29:45.770 --> 00:29:49.100
And that means you have to
create multidisciplinary teams

00:29:49.100 --> 00:29:52.250
where the policy people
and the delivery people

00:29:52.250 --> 00:29:55.910
are in the same space, which
means that as policy changes,

00:29:55.910 --> 00:29:57.990
you can change the
delivery mechanism.

00:29:57.990 --> 00:30:02.330
But also, and more importantly,
as the data changes,

00:30:02.330 --> 00:30:04.446
you can then make
the policy change.

00:30:04.446 --> 00:30:06.320
And you can start to
create a government that

00:30:06.320 --> 00:30:08.960
not only works for
its citizens, but is

00:30:08.960 --> 00:30:10.820
relevant for its citizens.

00:30:10.820 --> 00:30:12.845
And that relies on
excellent user research.

00:30:12.845 --> 00:30:15.950
It relies on using design
as your core methodology,

00:30:15.950 --> 00:30:16.850
as the core thing.

00:30:16.850 --> 00:30:20.030
The design of the
services are so important.

00:30:20.030 --> 00:30:22.010
And it also means
being big enough

00:30:22.010 --> 00:30:25.220
to work out that you need
to use continuous, iterative

00:30:25.220 --> 00:30:29.240
approaches that mean you
change as your people change.

00:30:29.240 --> 00:30:33.650
Remember, the system that we
built in 2008 that was there

00:30:33.650 --> 00:30:37.000
to work forever,
we've probably paid

00:30:37.000 --> 00:30:38.980
for it twice in all
the change control

00:30:38.980 --> 00:30:40.780
requests we had to bring in.

00:30:40.780 --> 00:30:43.830
This gets out of that
massive cost burden.

00:30:43.830 --> 00:30:47.230
It reduces the risk, and it
delivers government services

00:30:47.230 --> 00:30:49.670
that work for people.

00:30:49.670 --> 00:30:52.640
And so this is the mantra.

00:30:52.640 --> 00:30:53.690
Don't do it all yourself.

00:30:53.690 --> 00:30:54.440
You can't.

00:30:54.440 --> 00:30:55.790
Work with other people.

00:30:55.790 --> 00:30:58.160
As governments, we can work
with other governments.

00:30:58.160 --> 00:30:59.960
We don't compete.

00:30:59.960 --> 00:31:03.560
And if we start to focus on
what really works for citizens

00:31:03.560 --> 00:31:06.660
is the design and delivery
of great services,

00:31:06.660 --> 00:31:10.430
and that massively reduces
the size, the scale,

00:31:10.430 --> 00:31:14.780
and the cost of the tech
that runs behind it.

00:31:14.780 --> 00:31:18.710
We remove the friction,
because if you remove friction

00:31:18.710 --> 00:31:21.080
from services, things
work more effectively.

00:31:21.080 --> 00:31:23.030
More people will use them.

00:31:23.030 --> 00:31:25.510
More people will go to
use the digital service,

00:31:25.510 --> 00:31:29.070
and we will start to enable
the policy aim we started with,

00:31:29.070 --> 00:31:32.540
which was digital by default,
digital services that are so

00:31:32.540 --> 00:31:35.671
good people prefer to use them.

00:31:35.671 --> 00:31:37.420
And so that's how we're
going to make sure

00:31:37.420 --> 00:31:39.550
the government
keeps moving fast.

00:31:39.550 --> 00:31:42.220
And GovTech is a growth market.

00:31:42.220 --> 00:31:45.390
If you want to work and
interact with government,

00:31:45.390 --> 00:31:47.456
and you have components
for government,

00:31:47.456 --> 00:31:49.330
the digital marketplace,
the address of which

00:31:49.330 --> 00:31:51.280
is at the bottom
left, that's where

00:31:51.280 --> 00:31:54.380
you can apply to
deliver your services

00:31:54.380 --> 00:31:55.690
to the British government.

00:31:55.690 --> 00:31:58.960
And we want new,
innovative services

00:31:58.960 --> 00:32:01.846
that help us deliver better
services for our citizens.

00:32:01.846 --> 00:32:03.470
And because of the
way we've done this,

00:32:03.470 --> 00:32:05.440
and because of the
way we've built this,

00:32:05.440 --> 00:32:06.890
we now know we can do it.

00:32:06.890 --> 00:32:08.410
And those numbers
should help you

00:32:08.410 --> 00:32:10.512
realize that we're
serious about this,

00:32:10.512 --> 00:32:12.220
that if we're going
to get more than half

00:32:12.220 --> 00:32:15.665
of our business coming in on
new tech from small businesses,

00:32:15.665 --> 00:32:17.290
that's going to change
not only the way

00:32:17.290 --> 00:32:19.270
the British government
works, but also it's

00:32:19.270 --> 00:32:20.980
going to be a great
magnet for new tech

00:32:20.980 --> 00:32:23.439
to come into our country.

00:32:23.439 --> 00:32:24.980
So thank you very
much for listening.

00:32:24.980 --> 00:32:26.860
I'm open to questions.

00:32:26.860 --> 00:32:29.260
[APPLAUSE]

00:32:31.960 --> 00:32:35.910
[MUSIC PLAYING]

